WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:01.300
Why Finpoint?

00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:04.690
Your bank keeps a record of every payment you make.

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:08.055
What it can’t tell you is what each payment was really for.

00:00:08.933 --> 00:00:11.493
Say you spent $80 at the supermarket.

00:00:11.863 --> 00:00:16.933
$50 of that was groceries, and $30 was a birthday gift for a friend.

00:00:17.333 --> 00:00:19.783
Your bank shows one $80 payment.

00:00:20.073 --> 00:00:22.991
But to you, those are two very different things.

00:00:23.867 --> 00:00:30.027
Or you paid $90 for dinner with a friend, and they only sent their $60 share back a week later.

00:00:30.497 --> 00:00:33.337
Your bank shows two entries, a week apart:

00:00:33.487 --> 00:00:35.107
money out, and money in.

00:00:35.467 --> 00:00:37.942
What dinner really cost you was $30.

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:40.630
Finpoint closes that gap.

00:00:40.970 --> 00:00:46.630
It keeps your bank activity exactly as your bank reports it, and lets you explain it in your own words:

00:00:46.890 --> 00:00:48.790
groceries, $50.

00:00:49.030 --> 00:00:50.760
A gift, $30.

00:00:50.900 --> 00:00:53.134
Your share of dinner, $30.

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:59.740
Everything else in Finpoint, from spending charts to budgets, is built on those explanations.

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:02.970
So using Finpoint comes down to three things:

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:07.561
bring in your bank activity, explain it, and see where your money really went.

00:01:09.167 --> 00:01:11.481
Statements, Records and Allocations

00:01:12.081 --> 00:01:15.741
Finpoint uses three words for this, and they’re worth getting to know:

00:01:16.011 --> 00:01:18.478
Statements, Records and Allocations.

00:01:19.333 --> 00:01:24.303
A Statement is one entry of bank activity, exactly as your bank reports it.

00:01:24.593 --> 00:01:27.148
Like that $80 supermarket payment.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:30.570
A Record is your explanation of what happened.

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:33.480
Groceries for $50 is a Record.

00:01:33.770 --> 00:01:36.063
The gift for $30 is another.

00:01:36.933 --> 00:01:42.423
An Allocation connects a Statement to a Record, and says how much of the Statement belongs there.

00:01:42.923 --> 00:01:49.846
Here, $50 of the supermarket payment is allocated to Groceries, and the other $30 to the gift.

00:01:50.700 --> 00:01:52.020
That’s the first pattern:

00:01:52.170 --> 00:01:54.680
one Statement, split into several Records.

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.405
It’s how you untangle a mixed shopping trip.

00:01:58.267 --> 00:02:00.207
The second pattern goes the other way:

00:02:00.527 --> 00:02:03.387
several Statements, combined into one Record.

00:02:03.807 --> 00:02:11.677
Your $90 dinner payment and your friend’s $60 transfer both belong to one Dinner Record, which comes to your real share:

00:02:11.977 --> 00:02:13.048
$30.

00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:16.440
And most of the time, it’s simply one to one.

00:02:16.850 --> 00:02:21.374
A $12 lunch is one Statement, explained by one Lunch Record.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:24.712
Importing your bank statements

00:02:25.312 --> 00:02:27.782
Finpoint starts from your bank’s own records.

00:02:28.042 --> 00:02:31.262
You download them from your bank, then bring them into Finpoint.

00:02:32.133 --> 00:02:37.753
Finpoint can read exports from DBS, OCBC, UOB and Revolut.

00:02:38.163 --> 00:02:42.485
Depending on the bank, that’s a CSV file or an Excel spreadsheet.

00:02:43.367 --> 00:02:47.877
Each Statement belongs to an Account, which simply mirrors one of your bank accounts.

00:02:48.347 --> 00:02:59.624
Import your OCBC 360 Account and your UOB savings account, and Finpoint keeps their Statements apart, using the account details inside each file.

00:03:00.500 --> 00:03:04.340
Some exports, like Revolut’s, don’t say which account they came from.

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:08.215
For those, Finpoint asks you to pick or name the account.

00:03:09.067 --> 00:03:15.423
Download a CSV or Excel export from your bank, once for each account you want in Finpoint.

00:03:16.133 --> 00:03:19.134
Open Importer, choose your bank, and add your files.

00:03:19.867 --> 00:03:23.107
Choose Import, and check how many new Statements came in.

00:03:23.833 --> 00:03:26.533
Your new Statements are now waiting in Allocator.

00:03:26.783 --> 00:03:28.152
That’s where we’ll go next.

00:03:29.767 --> 00:03:31.043
Meet the Allocator

00:03:31.643 --> 00:03:34.883
Allocator is where you’ll spend most of your time in Finpoint.

00:03:35.273 --> 00:03:39.832
It lists every Statement that still has money left to explain, newest first.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:45.510
When only part of a Statement has been explained, Allocator shows how much is left.

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:49.327
Once a Statement is fully explained, it leaves the list.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:52.430
Two other pages help you look things up.

00:03:52.690 --> 00:03:56.410
Statements lists all your bank activity, explained or not.

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:58.730
Records lists your explanations.

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:01.584
Allocator only shows what’s still waiting.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:04.964
Explaining your first purchase

00:04:05.564 --> 00:04:07.744
Let’s explain a $12 lunch.

00:04:08.004 --> 00:04:10.994
In Allocator, click the lunch Statement to select it.

00:04:11.304 --> 00:04:13.656
A bar appears with what you can do next.

00:04:14.367 --> 00:04:15.747
Choose Create Record.

00:04:16.037 --> 00:04:20.643
A new Record opens with the Statement already attached, and its amount filled in.

00:04:21.367 --> 00:04:25.022
Give it a title, like Lunch, and pick a Category, like Dining Out.

00:04:25.900 --> 00:04:28.460
You’ll also see Treatment and Spending bucket.

00:04:28.840 --> 00:04:30.480
Leave those alone for now:

00:04:30.630 --> 00:04:33.964
your Category fills them in, and we’ll come back to them later.

00:04:34.833 --> 00:04:36.673
Choose Create Record to save.

00:04:37.003 --> 00:04:40.653
The lunch Statement leaves Allocator, because it’s fully explained.

00:04:42.267 --> 00:04:43.671
Splitting one payment

00:04:44.271 --> 00:04:46.611
Back to the $80 supermarket trip.

00:04:46.871 --> 00:04:50.037
Select it in Allocator, and choose Create Record.

00:04:50.767 --> 00:04:57.027
Call it Groceries, and change both the Record’s amount and the Statement’s Allocation to $50.

00:04:57.397 --> 00:05:00.525
The other $30 stays unexplained for now.

00:05:01.233 --> 00:05:06.252
Save, and the supermarket Statement stays in Allocator, with $30 left.

00:05:06.967 --> 00:05:12.687
Select it again, and create a second Record, Birthday gift, for the $30 left to allocate.

00:05:13.067 --> 00:05:15.270
Finpoint fills that amount in for you.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:21.219
Save, and the supermarket Statement leaves Allocator, because it’s now fully explained.

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:25.080
Now one Statement is explained by two Records:

00:05:25.360 --> 00:05:28.891
$50 of groceries, and a $30 gift.

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:32.804
Combining payments into one Record

00:05:33.404 --> 00:05:34.804
Now the shared dinner.

00:05:35.104 --> 00:05:37.494
In Allocator, select both Statements:

00:05:37.684 --> 00:05:41.594
the $90 you paid, and the $60 your friend sent back.

00:05:41.954 --> 00:05:43.614
The bar shows their total:

00:05:43.664 --> 00:05:44.710
$30.

00:05:45.433 --> 00:05:48.413
Choose Create Record, and call it Dinner with Sam.

00:05:48.783 --> 00:05:53.462
Both Statements are attached, and the Record comes to $30, your real share.

00:05:54.333 --> 00:05:57.843
Save, and two Statements are explained by a single Record.

00:05:58.243 --> 00:06:02.781
Your spending shows the $30 you really spent, not $90.

00:06:04.400 --> 00:06:05.782
Where your numbers come from

00:06:06.382 --> 00:06:09.832
With a few Records saved, your Dashboard starts to fill in.

00:06:10.242 --> 00:06:14.728
Everything on it is calculated from Records, never directly from Statements.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:18.740
That’s why importing alone leaves the Dashboard empty.

00:06:19.110 --> 00:06:21.883
A Statement only counts once you’ve explained it.

00:06:22.733 --> 00:06:27.393
And because it uses your explanations, the Dashboard shows what you really spent:

00:06:27.793 --> 00:06:32.520
$30 on dinner, not $90, and groceries apart from the gift.

00:06:33.400 --> 00:06:39.156
Your spending is broken down by Category, so the Categories you choose shape everything you see here.

00:06:40.767 --> 00:06:42.015
Pending Records

00:06:42.615 --> 00:06:45.825
Sometimes you know about spending before your bank shows all of it.

00:06:46.135 --> 00:06:48.187
Finpoint lets you record it anyway.

00:06:49.067 --> 00:06:56.067
Say you paid $200 for two concert tickets, and your friend Jo will pay you back $100 next week.

00:06:56.477 --> 00:07:01.869
You can already create the Record for your $100 share, from the $200 payment.

00:07:02.733 --> 00:07:06.893
The amounts don’t match yet, $100 against $200.

00:07:07.233 --> 00:07:10.572
Finpoint warns you, and saves the Record as Pending.

00:07:11.433 --> 00:07:15.674
Pending Records are marked in your Records list, so nothing gets forgotten.

00:07:16.533 --> 00:07:22.523
When Jo’s $100 arrives, select it in Allocator and choose Attach to Record.

00:07:23.033 --> 00:07:26.593
Finpoint suggests the Pending Record it matches exactly.

00:07:27.467 --> 00:07:29.727
The Record now combines both payments.

00:07:30.027 --> 00:07:33.596
Save, and because the amounts match, it’s no longer Pending.

00:07:34.467 --> 00:07:37.167
So a Record is Pending for one of two reasons.

00:07:37.457 --> 00:07:40.197
Its amounts don’t match yet, like the concert.

00:07:40.517 --> 00:07:46.087
Or it relies on bank activity you’re still expecting, like a sofa balance due on delivery.

00:07:46.597 --> 00:07:50.458
Either way, Pending means the Record isn’t fully confirmed yet.

00:07:52.067 --> 00:07:53.307
Pending Statements

00:07:53.907 --> 00:07:56.677
Some banks take days to show a card payment.

00:07:57.007 --> 00:08:00.447
If you want it explained today, create a Pending Statement:

00:08:00.757 --> 00:08:03.474
a placeholder for bank activity you’re expecting.

00:08:04.333 --> 00:08:07.553
On the Statements page, choose Create Pending Statement.

00:08:07.923 --> 00:08:11.542
Pick the account, then enter the amount and a short description.

00:08:12.267 --> 00:08:14.827
It appears in Allocator, marked Pending.

00:08:15.177 --> 00:08:17.267
Explain it like any other Statement.

00:08:17.637 --> 00:08:22.360
The Record counts straight away, but stays Pending while it relies on a placeholder.

00:08:23.067 --> 00:08:27.647
When the real Statement is imported, open Replace Pending in Allocator.

00:08:28.117 --> 00:08:33.046
Pick the placeholder, and Finpoint suggests the imported Statements it could become.

00:08:33.767 --> 00:08:34.897
Choose Replace.

00:08:35.217 --> 00:08:41.267
The Allocations move to the real Statement, the placeholder disappears, and nothing is counted twice.

00:08:42.867 --> 00:08:43.798
Categories

00:08:44.398 --> 00:08:47.728
Categories are the most important choice you make on a Record.

00:08:48.128 --> 00:08:52.135
They say what it was for, and the Dashboard breaks your spending down by them.

00:08:53.000 --> 00:08:55.590
Lunch and dinner with friends are Dining Out.

00:08:55.840 --> 00:08:58.100
The supermarket run is Groceries.

00:08:58.370 --> 00:09:01.430
The birthday gift goes under Gifts and Donations.

00:09:01.820 --> 00:09:06.658
Finpoint starts you with a sensible set, and you can rename them or add your own.

00:09:07.533 --> 00:09:10.233
Each Category also carries two defaults:

00:09:10.373 --> 00:09:12.463
a treatment, and a spending bucket.

00:09:12.803 --> 00:09:15.353
That’s why you could ignore those fields earlier.

00:09:15.583 --> 00:09:17.166
The Category fills them in.

00:09:18.767 --> 00:09:19.649
Treatments

00:09:20.249 --> 00:09:22.879
Treatment decides how the Dashboard counts a Record:

00:09:23.249 --> 00:09:27.076
as income, as spending, as saving, or not at all.

00:09:27.933 --> 00:09:29.543
Your salary is Income.

00:09:29.853 --> 00:09:35.223
Lunch and groceries are Spending, and so is a refund, which lowers what you spent.

00:09:35.673 --> 00:09:39.813
Moving $500 into savings is Saving/investment.

00:09:40.093 --> 00:09:44.632
And moving money between your own accounts is a Transfer, which isn’t counted at all.

00:09:45.500 --> 00:09:49.580
On the Dashboard, Income and Spending make up your totals and charts.

00:09:49.930 --> 00:09:54.420
Saving and investment is kept out of them, in its own section at the bottom.

00:09:54.770 --> 00:09:56.683
And Transfers don’t appear at all.

00:09:57.567 --> 00:09:59.747
Your Category sets the treatment for you.

00:10:00.057 --> 00:10:03.964
Change it on a single Record only when that Record is an exception.

00:10:05.567 --> 00:10:07.779
Spending buckets and monthly targets

00:10:08.379 --> 00:10:11.489
A spending bucket groups your spending for monthly planning.

00:10:11.849 --> 00:10:14.379
Think of it as a monthly budget that repeats.

00:10:14.719 --> 00:10:16.499
Finpoint starts you with four:

00:10:16.669 --> 00:10:19.513
Daily, Recurring, Irregular and Travel.

00:10:20.367 --> 00:10:22.127
Daily is the one to watch.

00:10:22.467 --> 00:10:28.019
It holds everyday spending, like food and transport, and the Dashboard paces it through the month.

00:10:28.900 --> 00:10:32.940
Give a bucket a monthly target, say $500 for Daily.

00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:36.715
You can set it for one month only, or from this month onward.

00:10:37.567 --> 00:10:41.177
The Dashboard then shows how this month is going against the target.

00:10:41.507 --> 00:10:43.197
Targets don’t move any money.

00:10:43.387 --> 00:10:44.956
They’re simply a comparison.

00:10:46.567 --> 00:10:48.328
Budgets for trips and events

00:10:48.928 --> 00:10:54.233
A Budget is for spending with its own start and end, like a week in Bali, or a wedding.

00:10:55.100 --> 00:10:58.210
Create one with a name, an amount and its dates.

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:03.910
Leave Automatic attach on, and Records dated inside the period are attached for you:

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:07.368
the ones you already have, and new ones as you add them.

00:11:08.233 --> 00:11:11.393
A Budget is its own small dashboard for that period:

00:11:11.683 --> 00:11:14.273
what you’ve spent, what’s left, and your pace.

00:11:14.643 --> 00:11:17.828
It’s separate from your monthly Dashboard, and doesn’t change it.

00:11:19.433 --> 00:11:21.027
Backups and Google Drive

00:11:21.627 --> 00:11:25.047
Finpoint keeps your data in this browser, on this device.

00:11:25.447 --> 00:11:31.335
There’s no Finpoint database, and your finances never pass through our servers, so we never see them.

00:11:32.200 --> 00:11:35.820
That means keeping a copy is up to you, and there are two ways.

00:11:36.220 --> 00:11:37.950
The first is a backup file.

00:11:38.170 --> 00:11:44.229
Download it from the Data page whenever you like, and restore it later, on this device or a new one.

00:11:45.100 --> 00:11:46.950
The second is Google Drive.

00:11:47.260 --> 00:11:55.032
Connect it once, and Finpoint keeps a copy in a hidden folder in your own Drive, automatically, so your devices stay in step.

00:11:55.900 --> 00:11:59.278
Either way, your data only goes to places you control.

00:12:00.900 --> 00:12:01.922
Your routine

00:12:02.522 --> 00:12:04.212
Here’s a routine that works.

00:12:04.362 --> 00:12:06.042
Import your latest statements.

00:12:06.282 --> 00:12:08.322
Explain what’s new in Allocator.

00:12:08.512 --> 00:12:09.842
Check anything Pending.

00:12:10.042 --> 00:12:11.452
Glance at your Dashboard.

00:12:11.502 --> 00:12:12.590
And keep a backup.

00:12:13.467 --> 00:12:16.847
With Google Drive connected, that happens automatically.

00:12:17.227 --> 00:12:20.347
If not, download a backup file every so often.

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:24.880
And whenever you’re unsure, open Help from any page or form.

00:12:25.310 --> 00:12:28.351
Every chapter of this guide is there, with pictures.
